  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLatest GOES Water Vapor imagery continues to reflect the slow\napproach of an upper-level low from western New York. Meanwhile,\nthe latest SPC Mesoscale Analysis places a strengthening surface\ncyclone just off the coast of Cape Cod, extending an inverted\ntrough westward across the northern portion of our CWA. Aloft,\nstrong divergent flow enhances the necessary forcing to sustain\nperiods of rain showers currently seen throughout eastern New \nYork and western New England. Enhanced low-level convergence \nassociated with the inverted surface trough, along with an \nappreciable band of FGEN along the baroclinic zone at 850 mb, \nhas supported the organized rain bands currently pivoting \nthrough portions of the Eastern Catskills, Mohawk Valley, and \nSouthern Adirondacks. In these areas, 0.75\" to 1.50\" of rain \nhas fallen over the 24hrs per MRMS as of 00 UTC/08.\n\nAs the closed low aloft gradually shifts into New England\ntonight, the bands of steady rain will shift south and eastward\ninto the Capital District, eastern Catskills, Taconics, western\nMA and southern VT. While rainfall rates up to 0.15-0.30\" are\nstill favored under the more organized rain bands, the closed\nlow will become more progressive. This should limit the \nduration of moderate rainfall rates. Storm total rainfall \namounts of 0.50 - 2\" are likely for areas mainly near and north\nof I-90 by 12 UTC Sunday when most of the steady rain should \nexit to our east. Despite areas of 1-2 inches of rain, only\nexpected isolated instances of poor drainage flooding in low-\nlying and typical trouble spots. Widespread flooding is not\nexpected given the recent, very dry stretch of weather.\n\nHeights rise in the wake of our exiting closed low tomorrow \nresulting in dry conditions and increasing afternoon sun. \nSufficient mixing courtesy of increasing subsidence under the\nincoming ridge will also favor breezy westerly winds with gusts\nup to 20-25mph and high temperatures rising into the mid/upper \n40s to low 50s across higher elevations with mid 50s to pockets \nof 60 in the valleys. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMultiple shortwaves are anticipated to track through the region\nbeginning tomorrow night through Wednesday, leading to the\npotential for additional rounds of scattered, light, and non-\nimpactful showers. That said, the most potent disturbances will\nrotate through tomorrow night and Monday, leading to the next best\nchances for precipitation. Most of the showers look to be\nconfined to lake-adjacent and higher terrain areas as west to\nwest-northwesterly flow will induce a lake response amid cold\nair advection. And while most of these showers will again\npredominantly be rain, a few snowflakes could mix into the\nAdirondacks tomorrow night and Monday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nMedium- to long-range deterministic and ensemble guidance\ncontinues to align in the portrayal of a large-scale trough\ndeveloping towards the end of the upcoming week. According to\nthe latest NAEFs, by Thursday, upper-level; mid-level; and low-\nlevel heights look to fall to roughly 1-2 STDEVs below normal.\nThe direct impact of this outcome will be temperatures dipping\nbelow normal Thursday and Friday, though only by about 0.5 to 1\nSTDEV as of this update. \n\nPrecipitation chances during this time are highly uncertain.\nConsequently, precipitation types are also highly uncertain.\nWhile there is a storm system being resolved in the guidance as\npotentially impacting the region, possibly as early as Thursday,\nthere is little agreement not only between the various models, \nbut their individual run-to-run solutions in its evolution. The\nevolution and resulting outcome of this storm is going to be \nhighly dependent on the strength, orientation, and timing of the\naforementioned developing trough. The uncertainty that these\ndetails retain at this great a lead time justify the very low\nconfidence in that storm system. However, we will continue to\nmonitor trends closely over the coming week.",
